WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.560
Film Courage: Can we go back to your script
reading days?

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.730
First off, what qualifies someone as a script
reader?

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.230
How do they know you are ready to be doing
coverage?

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.610
Adam William Ward, Director/Co-Screenwriter/Actor
of WALLY GOT WASTED: It’s so funny…nothing,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.210
nothing qualified me at all.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.270
I got the job and they started shoving scripts
in my hand.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.780
I had no qualifications for that whatsoever
and that’s the truth.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.210
I got better as I went.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.740
I’m sure my first synopsis was kind of like
‘what the heck is this?’

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.800
But I’m doing three a day so eventually
I am getting better and better and better.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.510
And that’s the thing too nothing qualifies
you in this industry.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:36.230
If you want to make films, if you want act
or whatever you want to do, there’s no qualifications.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.280
It’s not like every other job.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.540
But at the same time the problem is the whole
world…it is the same when it comes to experience.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.609
It’s not the same as the qualification of
“I went to Harvard.

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:54.870
You are qualified to do this job.”

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.069
It’s “Oh…you’ve done 15 movies.

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:03.289
I’ve seen the qualifications in the way
that I put a camera on you and you’re not

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:04.289
going to get nervous.

00:01:04.289 --> 00:01:05.890
You are going to deliver.”

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.229
And all that kind of stuff.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:09.659
It’s very different in that way.

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:15.689
And really the people that I see succeed are
the people that never give up, who keep learning

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:20.469
and getting better and better and it’s very
rare to see that because certain people get

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:25.530
to certain age and they want to have babies
and do certain things and certain things are

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.420
pulling them in certain directions.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.549
And it’s very hard to do the same thing
if you haven’t hit the idea of what you

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:35.609
imagined.

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:38.030
But a lot of people come here and say “I’ll
give it a year.”

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:44.020
But if you wanted to be the CEO of Apple would
you give it a year?

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.649
Because that’s what this is.

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:47.950
If you’re a lead in the TV show, that’s
what this is.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.030
You are a CEO of that company now.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.000
You’re the face of that TV show.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.039
Everyone is putting millions of dollars behind
you so you better be good, really good.

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:05.260
And have worked your way up and be the CEO
of that company because that’s what you

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.179
are at that point.

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:09.220
And the same thing with the director in a
movie.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.182
Because if they give you 20 or 30 million
dollars or a hundred million dollars to make

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:15.830
a movie I mean the money speaks for itself.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:20.120
You are a CEO of that company, you are completely
in charge of making that a good movie or a

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.110
bad movie at that point.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.970
It takes that time to get that good and people
to believe in you.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.360
Film Courage: So you didn’t have the qualification
to have what you thought you would have had

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.560
to be a script reader or you were just surprised
that they entrusted you?

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.030
Adam: It was mind-blowing to me.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.920
Because before that point me and my brother
were writing scripts back in Oregon when I

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.780
had no connections whatsoever and we would
meet a guy who could get it to somebody else.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.620
I’m now on the other side of it all reading
the scripts.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.080
And I realized that when I was writing them
they were handing them to the intern that

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.310
had absolutely no qualifications and might
just not like my script.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.190
Mostly anyone reading a script has absolutely
no qualifications whatsoever.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.920
They read it and if they like it because of
their opinion, it goes up the ladder to someone

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.700
else who has more experience and more experience
and then eventually someone who maybe can

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.590
really look at a script and dissect the whole
thing.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:22.090
But you have to get through all these other
layers of people who really don’t know what

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.090
they are doing.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:29.480
It better be so good that they read it and
be so intrigued by it but it could also be

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.600
you ran into the right people (three people)
that loved your script or loved your idea

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.600
or whatever.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.540
So mostly everyone reading a script has no
qualifications in the beginning at a studio

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.320
so the bottom layer people have no qualifications.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.970
Film Courage: Would you ever stop reading
a script or your job was to read it all the

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:49.970
way through?

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:55.290
Adam: You are doing three scripts a day so
if I read and it’s just horrible the first

00:03:55.290 --> 00:04:00.320
twenty pages I’m going to try to sum up
the script the best way possible.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.740
I’ll read the last 15 pages and most of
the people in the office will tell you that

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.810
like somebody in there was like you should
ready the first ten and the last ten, decide

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.720
if you like it and then you might read it.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.270
Otherwise that’s all that matters.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:17.609
If it’s not good there get rid of it.

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:20.660
I ended up doing that sometimes and I felt
bad for people.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.940
If it would bad it was bad.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:29.530
That’s the thing like when you have a stack
next to you that is 20 scripts, not everyone

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:30.590
is going to get an opportunity.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.750
So with the first 20 pages there’s no bad…the
whole thing has to be amazing.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:37.570
If the first 20 pages are bad then your script
is done.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.530
It’s not going to get to the next level.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.731
And actually once I made a film distribution
companies tell me that first 10 minutes of

00:04:45.731 --> 00:04:49.949
your movie better be amazing because people
will turn it off on these streaming platforms

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:50.949
.

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:54.060
So you better put a lot of your budget in
that first ten minutes, have a chase scene,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:05:01.100
have other scenes, whatever captures them,
high drama, something to drag that audience

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.460
in because everyone’s attention span is
so short nowadays.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:10.380
Film Courage: And then what was coverage like
even if it was just the first ten and the

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:14.810
last ten, what are you filling up on a piece
of paper of coverage.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.060
Adam: So I wouldn’t write on the script.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:24.840
I would write a one-page synopsis and try
to basically make an outline of the script.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.910
Really if you did your own coverage you’d
be saving people a lot of time to be honest

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:33.460
with you because Hollywood people want your
movie in one sentence and then if they are

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.540
intrigued by their one sentence then they
will want to read a paragraph.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.800
So if you write one sentence that sums up
your movie, great.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.590
Then write a paragraph that sums up your movie,
great.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:46.500
Then have a one-page synopsis that sums up
your movie, better.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.800
And if you handed that off to somebody they
would love you to death.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.639
And you can master getting each one of those
great because the intern is going to write

00:05:56.639 --> 00:05:59.820
your one-page synopsis when you could have
done it yourself in a way.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.919
Or your one-liner or your one paragraph or
whatever.

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:05.681
So that’s kind of how it is.

00:06:05.681 --> 00:06:08.750
Film Courage: And so you then you got HANGOVER,
it landed on your desk?

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.120
Adam: They already loved HANGOVER, I didn’t
green light HANGOVER.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.370
They already had HANGOVER and liked it.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.310
That was one of the two scripts they were
developing at the time.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.740
It was that script and another called MANWHICH.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.620
And they were like these are the two movies
that we’re going to make.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.830
Check them out eventually.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:31.200
But at the time all of the excitement was
over the MANWHICH movie not over the HANGOVER.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.340
And that was the next movie they were going
to make not HANGOVER and I was constantly

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.340
like…

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:37.680
And maybe Todd always knew he was going to
do HANGOVER.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.139
I don’t know I wasn’t really talking to
Todd that much.

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.050
He came into the office once in a blue moon.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:48.419
Scott Butnik was his partner at the time so
I had a lot more interaction with him.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:54.389
And for me when I read it I thought it was
very funny and it’s a page turner because

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:58.800
it’s a mystery they are trying to find their
friend.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.210
It was all solid, it was good.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:03.790
And someone else actually wrote the script
and Todd and his partner re-wrote it.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.770
So they didn’t actually write the first
draft of it.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.370
And they went on to do some other movies and
stuff.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:15.680
Film Courage: Eventually you saw that you
could become that guy or that girl and you’d

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.699
be making a nice living but you would be doing
exactly what you want to do?

00:07:19.699 --> 00:07:24.401
Did you have a plan to get out or was it one
day you just threw a bunch of papers up in

00:07:24.401 --> 00:07:25.670
the air and walked out?

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.010
What happened?

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:37.050
Adam: No, I was only at each place for like
three months and both places it was kind of

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.280
a short term thing anyway so it wasn’t like
I had to go “Oh I’m leaving.”

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.040
The time is up that you were supposed to be
here anyway.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.550
That was nice in a way.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.190
I didn’t get promoted you know what I mean?

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:51.630
It’s very hard for people to turn down money
when you first get here so for a lot of these

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:57.860
people it was like Oh now I’ve got $50,000…now
I’ve got $100,000…now I’m making $300,000

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:58.860
a year.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:07:59.860
I have a wife.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:01.000
How am I supposed to leave this job?”

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.000
You know what I mean?

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.040
You are money dependent on this job.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:08.130
And I’m lucky they didn’t offer me a position
because I might have tried to embrace it which

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:14.290
a lot of people do embrace their 9-to-5 job
and give up on their dreams.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:20.710
And I’m lucky that I was raised by my father
and by my mom and my dad always instilled

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.360
in me you can do anything you want to do.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.370
You just have to go for it and not take “no”
for an answer.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.960
That’s kind of my mentality with life.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:29.960
You’ve got to go do it.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.460
Film Courage: Did you see that in him?

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:32.890
Adam: In my dad?

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:36.199
My dad actually was a musician back in the
day.

00:08:36.199 --> 00:08:43.110
And he was almost the lead singer in Aerosmith
when Aerosmith was having drug problems and

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:47.970
stuff and there was a guy named Arthur Mann
basically that put a contract in front of

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:56.180
my dad and was like “I want you to perform
in every club, bar, stage from here to China.”

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:05.269
And my dad had…my mom was pregnant at the
time and he had a real job and he had to decide…he

00:09:05.269 --> 00:09:08.459
gave it up and he became a family man really.

00:09:08.459 --> 00:09:13.019
And the same contract Arthur Mann gave it
to Bon Jovi.

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:20.569
So Bon Jovi ended up becoming Bon Jovi and
my dad ended up being a dad and shortly afterward

00:09:20.569 --> 00:09:22.040
my dad got in a bad car accident.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:27.000
So he ended up being a stay-at-home-dad and
my mom worked and so my house was very reversed

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.170
in the 80’s where the mom stayed home.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:35.259
Where with my dad he was a stay-at-home-dad
so it was very different in that way.

00:09:35.259 --> 00:09:40.860
But it’s kind of crazy though because he
trained my sister how to sing and so my sister

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.259
is ZZ Ward (she’s a big time singer).

00:09:43.259 --> 00:09:46.329
And so the next generation still made it as
a singer.

00:09:46.329 --> 00:09:50.869
She signed with Hollywood Records (which is
Disney) and she’s working on her third album

00:09:50.869 --> 00:09:52.259
now.

00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:57.959
So he passed up his music dream but she accomplished
it so it’s kind of nice to see that in full

00:09:57.959 --> 00:09:58.459
circle for him.

